  "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-18)).$ Notice that $-3-(-18)=15,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Case II gives a true statement. Indeed, $12∣(-3-(-15)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-3-(-15))÷12=12÷12=1\n$$\n\n- Case III gives a false statement because $12∤(-3-(-9)).$ Notice that $-3-(-9)=6,$ which is not divisible by $12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$-15$ only.\"",
